#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521456 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064379 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2dbfe46-55f0-5d6a-b410-164f0aa2345f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Related Rate With a Circle: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9aa873a6-fa87-5112-b328-59b790bb02cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521114} {:answer/id #uuid "a28cf962-96ea-5fd2-a648-e08c58efd301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521129} {:answer/id #uuid "a6084d40-a60b-5595-86d6-684fff85cf4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521135} {:answer/id #uuid "a75bdbec-56f0-53b5-95b6-f2d3c36945c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521139} {:answer/id #uuid "ec3d86cc-339a-5c68-b539-6749321e41e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6084d40-a60b-5595-86d6-684fff85cf4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521135} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8c5f3c7-fd49-5b7c-9172-09702c7f16cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ac5cd87-de15-5914-9f66-8d76ea6b7dee" :question/math-academy-id "q-2555" :question/problem "![](images/89/8946fc7f3b9a087304d893d69196f8462913974aab552b05a442a7460a619bef.png)\n\nWater leaking onto a floor forms a circular pool. The radius of the pool increases at a rate of $6\\,\\text{cm/min}.$ How fast is the area of the pool increasing at the moment when the radius is $\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}?$" :question/worked-solution "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=\\frac{2}{\\pi },$ and we are also told that  $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=6.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the area formula for the circle and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=6$ and $r=\\frac{2}{\\pi },$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =6\\cdot 2\\pi (\\frac{2}{\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the pool is increasing at a rate of $24{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064381 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dabb3127-172e-5a68-8e27-d22057f8ea8e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Related Rate With a Sphere: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f48b03f4-de1f-5518-8d7b-f0622fb8986b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296561} {:answer/id #uuid "8c96d101-996a-5077-8061-aff0a5c888fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c96d101-996a-5077-8061-aff0a5c888fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521082} :answer-field/id #uuid "3697c49e-740a-5aaa-a707-4701abf7681d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb5b4de1-d892-5870-99f2-1c26e26d0697" :question/math-academy-id "q-315860" :question/problem "![](images/e8/e850cd14f3b4f791c79c136d0fc799fd17fdc23eeab37e67ad2811f3ff7b6d0e.png)\n\nA snowball is melting in the sun and slowly shrinking. Determine the rate at which the surface area of the snowball is decreasing, given that the radius is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{in/s}$ and its surface area at that moment is $100\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe surface area of the snowball is decreasing at a rate of \n{{field-1}} ${\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the snowball and let $A$ be its surface area. Both $r$ and $A$ change with time. In particular, we are given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{in/s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ when $A=100\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the formula for the surface area of a sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 8\\pi r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then calculate the value of $r$ using $A=100\\pi .$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 100\\pi  & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 25 & ={r}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince radius cannot be negative, we have $r=5.$\n\nWe now substitute $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{5}$ and $r=5$ into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 8\\pi \\cdot 5 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the surface area of the snowball is decreasing at a rate of $8\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064379 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2dbfe46-55f0-5d6a-b410-164f0aa2345f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Related Rate With a Circle: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db110e87-ecff-5df6-86c8-2144e4e980f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db110e87-ecff-5df6-86c8-2144e4e980f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521254} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c7ad603-536b-5d70-82a1-a923240f239d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d564be6e-5836-50c8-903c-6a70a3ceed94" :question/math-academy-id "q-344736" :question/problem "![](images/bc/bc7e248783ccf6b1eda95a1c2c80300e48d8cdb48f39ea07296ed3bf51bfaa29.png)\n\nA small, circular puddle evaporates on a floor. If the puddle’s area is decreasing at a rate of $32\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min},$ calculate the rate of change of its circumference at the moment when the radius is $8\\,\\text{cm}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe circumference of the puddle is decreasing at a rate of \n{{field-1}} $\\text{cm}/\\text{min}.$" :question/worked-solution "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=8,$ and we are also told that  $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-32\\pi .$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $r=8\\,\\text{cm},$ $C=2\\pi (8)=16\\pi \\text{ cm}.$ Hence, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-32\\pi $ and $C=16\\pi ,$ and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-32\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{16\\pi }{2\\pi } \\\\ -32\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 8 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the circumference is decreasing at a rate of $4\\pi \\text{ cm}\\text{/min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317539 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064381 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dabb3127-172e-5a68-8e27-d22057f8ea8e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Related Rate With a Sphere: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4804743-754e-52a4-ac46-58a24afecd98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4804743-754e-52a4-ac46-58a24afecd98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316482} :answer-field/id #uuid "87f30560-b7a8-5fe7-a866-713cb0a11c66" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a6cc0140-a8eb-5088-99b0-8b7a894e4ee5" :question/math-academy-id "q-344751" :question/problem "![](images/28/287add661cff085d1090295f67f87ee80eeaa3d8301ee6632ab54a812a6cd119.png)\n\nA spherical hailstone is melting uniformly, causing its radius to change at a rate of $\\frac{1}{6\\pi }\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/s}.$ If the radius at a certain moment is $15\\,\\text{mm},$ what is the rate at which the surface area is decreasing?\n\nThe surface area of the hailstone is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the hailstone and $A$ its surface area. Both $r$ and $A$ change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{6\\pi }\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=15\\,\\text{mm}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the surface area formula for the sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}8\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{6\\pi }$ and $r=15.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{6\\pi }\\cdot 8\\pi (15) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the surface area of the hailstone is decreasing at a rate of $20{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521513 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064381 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dabb3127-172e-5a68-8e27-d22057f8ea8e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Related Rate With a Sphere: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae33c09f-da88-5496-ac0c-693ebc91e98f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae33c09f-da88-5496-ac0c-693ebc91e98f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521152} :answer-field/id #uuid "e74b5676-bbc7-5018-8226-6434bdbb2081" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7842bd80-84fe-5d31-bbd1-38dac51d5756" :question/math-academy-id "q-344787" :question/problem "A spherical hailstone is growing as it moves through a storm cloud. At a particular moment, the rate of increase in the volume of the hailstone is numerically equal to three times the rate of increase in its surface area. What is the radius of the hailstone at that moment? Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the hailstone, $V$ the volume, and $A$ its surface area. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=3\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the volume $V$ and the surface area $A$ of the sphere. Since $A=4\\pi {r}^{2}$ and $V=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{r}^{3},$ the relationship between $V$ and $A$ is\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{r}^{3}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{(\\frac{A}{4\\pi })}^{3/2}=\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{3(4\\pi )^{1/2}}=\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}A}(\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{{A}^{1/2}}{4\\sqrt{\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=3\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{{A}^{1/2}}{4\\sqrt{\\pi }} \\\\ {A}^{1/2} & =12\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\ A & =(12\\sqrt{\\pi })^{2} \\\\ A & =144\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $A=4\\pi {r}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\pi {r}^{2} & =144\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =36 \\\\ r & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
